eople were doing  our kind of problems solving them in a a  you know a functional programming way  there's been relational programming in  the functional language community with  things like list comprehensions and  monad comprehensions in in languages  like haskell  um  that you can kind of see the  relationship but the the problem with  that is that those looked alien to  people that were writing sql queries  so um  well they they look kind of similar but  there are very few sql programmers that  were prepared to cross over to haskell  plus it didn't run on a distributed  system  so what we've tried to do in morale is  kind of create these links more of an  explicit link between the good things of  the query system which is query  optimizers and so forth and the ability  to run on you know large parallel  systems  uh integrate that more closely with the  the functional programming language type  system and and and so forth  so i see morel is this opportunity to  unify  the all of these various ideas  and  i encourage you to try it out and come  and work with me  so in summary morale is a functional  query language  it has a rich type system  inherited from its  functional programming  uh parentage um it's as concise as sql  um but it is also too incomplete it's a  general-purpose language  it combines relational query  optimization and  lambda  functional programming optimization  techniques  it executes it natively executes in a  java interpreter but it can generate  programs for  data parallel engines  so  i don't think we have time for questions  here  but i'll be outside the door and we'll  happily chat for many many hours as long  as you guys want to talk so  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

